<section class="brain-spine-section">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">What Is Sciatica?</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <p>Sciatica is pain that travels along the path of the sciatic nerve, the longest and thickest nerve in the body. It is formed by nerve roots leaving the spine at the L4 to S3 levels, passes through the pelvis and down the back of the leg to the foot. When one or more of those nerve roots is compressed, irritated or inflamed, the pain radiates from the lower back through the buttock and down the affected leg.</p>
  <p>It matters that sciatica is a symptom rather than a diagnosis. The leg pain is a signal that something is affecting the nerve pathway and the point of assessment is to find out what. Classical Ayurveda describes this presentation as Gridhrasi, named for the altered gait it produces and connects it to a disturbance of Vata in the lower body. Common underlying causes include a <a href="/pages/ayurvedic-slip-disc-treatment-in-hyderabad" style="color:#3c8dbc">herniated disc</a>, narrowing of the spinal canal, tightness of the deep buttock muscle and age-related change in the lower spine.</p>
  <p>The pain may be sharp, burning or shooting along the nerve path. Some patients also have numbness, tingling or weakness in the affected leg. Symptoms often worsen with prolonged sitting or standing and with movements such as bending forward. Sciatica usually affects one leg and less commonly both, which needs closer evaluation.</p>
</section>

<section class="brain-spine-section" style="background:#f8f9fa">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Types of Sciatica</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <div class="cs-types-grid">
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>True Sciatica</h4>
      <p>This follows direct compression or irritation of the sciatic nerve or the nerve roots feeding it. It is typically caused by a herniated disc, a narrowed spinal canal or another structural change in the lower spine that puts mechanical pressure on the nerve. The pain follows a defined band down the leg and comes with neurological signs such as numbness, tingling or weakness.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Referred Sciatica</h4>
      <p>Here the pain travels along the same route without the nerve itself being compressed. It arises from problems in nearby structures such as the joint at the base of the spine, the small joints of the lower spine or the surrounding soft tissue. The pattern can mimic true sciatica but examination shows no nerve deficit. The distinction is clinically important, since the cause and the correct treatment differ.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Sciatica on Both Sides</h4>
      <p>Pain radiating down both legs at once is less common than one-sided sciatica. It may point to a central disc herniation, significant narrowing of the spinal canal or compression of the bundle of nerves at the base of the spine. Symptoms on both sides need prompt and careful evaluation rather than conservative treatment started on assumption.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card">
      <h4>Acute and Chronic Sciatica</h4>
      <p>Acute sciatica develops suddenly, often after heavy lifting or a sudden twist and typically lasts from a few days to several weeks. Chronic sciatica persists beyond eight to twelve weeks or recurs repeatedly over months. The shift from acute to chronic may involve continuing structural compression, sustained inflammation or changes in how the nervous system processes pain.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-type-card" style="grid-column:1/-1;max-width:420px;margin:0 auto">
      <h4>Sciatica from Muscle Entrapment</h4>
      <p>Here the piriformis muscle, a deep muscle in the buttock, tightens or spasms and presses on the sciatic nerve as it passes beneath or through it. This produces buttock pain and leg symptoms aggravated by prolonged sitting, stairs or turning the hip. It is worth identifying because the compression is outside the spine altogether so treatment aimed at the spine would miss it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="brain-spine-section">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">Signs and Symptoms of Sciatica</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <p>Sciatica presents as a combination of pain, altered sensation and loss of function. Commonly reported signs include:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Shooting or burning pain:</strong> A sharp, electric or burning pain travelling from the lower back or buttock down the back of the thigh into the calf or foot, following the course of the nerve. It may intensify on coughing, sneezing or straining.</li>
    <li><strong>Numbness and tingling:</strong> Reduced sensation or a pins and needles feeling in the affected leg, foot or toes. Some areas may feel numb while others are unusually sensitive to touch.</li>
    <li><strong>Leg weakness:</strong> Reduced strength in the affected leg, which may cause difficulty lifting the foot, climbing stairs or keeping balance. Weakness indicates that the motor fibres of the nerve are involved so it is assessed rather than simply treated for pain.</li>
    <li><strong>Pain worse on sitting:</strong> Sitting raises pressure on the lower back discs and can further compress the affected nerve roots. Many patients find that standing or walking brings partial relief.</li>
    <li><strong>One-sided symptoms:</strong> Sciatica usually affects one leg, on the same side as the affected nerve root. Involvement of both legs is possible and warrants prompt evaluation.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="brain-spine-section" style="background:#f8f9fa">
  <h2 class="brain-spine-title">When Sciatica Needs Urgent Assessment</h2>
  <div class="brain-spine-divider"></div>
  <p>Most sciatica is managed without urgency. A small number of presentations are different and need immediate medical assessment rather than a course of conservative treatment. Seek urgent care, the same day, if any of the following occur:</p>
  <div class="cauda-equina-warning">
    <p><strong>Seek immediate medical assessment if you experience:</strong></p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px!important">&bull; Weakness in the leg that is getting worse or difficulty lifting the front of the foot</p>
    <p>&bull; Numbness around the inner thighs, buttocks or genital area</p>
    <p>&bull; Any change in bladder or bowel control, including difficulty passing urine or loss of control</p>
    <p>&bull; Sudden severe symptoms in both legs together</p>
    <p>&bull; Sciatic pain following significant trauma such as a fall or an accident or occurring alongside fever or unexplained weight loss</p>
  </div>
  <p>These can indicate compression of the bundle of nerves at the base of the spine, known as cauda equina syndrome or another condition needing surgical assessment. In those situations delay affects the outcome so the correct step is emergency assessment at a hospital rather than booking an elective consultation. Patients already under care at KSAC Hospitals are asked to report new or worsening weakness or any change in bladder or bowel function without waiting for their next appointment.</p>
</section>
